    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `pytest_runtest_makereport`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `pytest_runtest_makereport` function is a pytest hook that modifies the test report generation process. It is designed to track the outcome of each stage of a test (setup, call, teardown) and store this information in a dictionary associated with the test item. The function takes two parameters: `item`, which is the test item being executed, and `call`, which is a generator that yields the test report. The function yields control back to pytest to generate the initial report, then asserts that the report's `when` attribute is set, indicating which stage of the test the report corresponds to. It then creates an empty dictionary if one does not already exist in the test item's stash under the key `tmppath_result_key`. This dictionary is used to store the pass/fail status of each test stage. Finally, the function returns the modified report. The function relies on pytest's hook mechanism and the `setdefault` method of dictionaries to manage the stash. It does not handle any specific errors or edge cases, and its primary side effect is modifying the test item's stash to include the test stage outcomes.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
